Vertex AI → Tuning has two options:

1. Managed Tuning 🤖

Google manages most of the tuning process for you.

You mainly provide:

A base model
Your training examples
Basic configuration

Then Vertex AI handles much of the infrastructure and tuning process.

======

Example: Bakery Assistant

Suppose you want an AI to always give answers in your bakery's style.

Training examples:

Input:

How can I increase cake sales?

Expected Output:

Promote birthday cake combos, offer pre-order discounts, and use WhatsApp marketing.

Input:

How can I reduce food waste?

Expected Output:

Track daily sales and prepare production quantities based on demand.

You provide many such examples.

Your Training Data
        ↓
Managed Tuning
        ↓
Google manages tuning process
        ↓
Tuned Model
        ↓
Your Bakery AI
Simple meaning

You give the data → Google does most of the tuning work.



======


2. Custom Tuning ⚙️

With Custom Tuning, you have more control over the tuning process.

You can control things such as:

Training configuration
Hyperparameters
Learning rate
Number of training steps/epochs
Other model training settings
Your Training Data
        ↓
You configure training settings
        ↓
Custom Tuning
        ↓
You control more parameters
        ↓
Tuned Model
Example

Suppose you are building a specialized Textile Quality AI.

You have thousands of examples:

Fabric Image/Data → Defect Type
─────────────────────────────────
Example 1 → Hole
Example 2 → Stain
Example 3 → Color Variation
Example 4 → Thread Defect

You may want more control over how the model learns from this data.

So you use Custom Tuning.

You give the data + configure more training settings → Google runs the infrastructure.

========
Imagine you want to customize a cake 🎂.

|             | Managed Tuning               | Custom Tuning                |
| ----------- | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Your role   | Give requirements            | Control more details         |
| Bakery role | Does most of the work        | You specify exact settings   |
| Difficulty  | Easier                       | More advanced                |
| Control     | Less                         | More                         |
| Best for    | Beginners / common use cases | Advanced / specialized needs |


=========

Cake example

Managed Tuning:

"I want a chocolate birthday cake."

The baker decides many of the technical details.

Custom Tuning:

"Use 500g flour, 200g chocolate, bake at this temperature, and follow these specific settings."

You have much more control.

=======

or your Business Consultant AI

Currently, you likely do not need fine-tuning just to make it answer business questions.

Your current approach can be:

User Question
      ↓
Gemini / Vertex AI Agent
      ↓
Instructions / System Prompt
      ↓
Business Advice

Fine-tuning becomes useful when you have a large number of high-quality examples and want the model to consistently learn a particular behavior, style, format, or specialized task.

